  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis shows high pressure now\nsitting off the coast of the mid-Atlantic while broad ridging\ncontinues to expand over much of the CONUS' southern tier. Winds\nacross the area are now out of the south to southeast and\ntemperatures have climbed a few degrees from yesterday into the\nmid to upper 80s. Dews have also jumped as well, into the low to\nmid 60s.\n\nTemperatures peak tomorrow and Friday as flow turns more\nsouthwesterly and low-level thicknesses peak, with highs in the\nlow to mid 90s. Dew points also peak on Friday, resulting in\nmaximum heat index values returning to between 100-105. At this\ntime the odds of issuing a Heat Advisory look to be less than\n10%, but trends will continue to be monitored.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mid- level subsidence aloft will keep \nprecipitation chances very subdued this afternoon despite the \nuptick in instability, although cannot completely rule out an \nisolated shower or two along typical sound and sea breeze \nconvergence zones. Shortwave approaches from the central plains \ntomorrow, weakening the ridge and aiding in a slightly more \nfavorable environment for isolated to widely scattered showers \nand thunderstorms.\n\nFriday the high continues to shift offshore and a front sweeps \nsouth through the Mid-Atlantic region. Ahead of this front, \ncontinuous warm, moist advection in southerly flow will help \nbuild PWATs to near 2\". This will bring scattered to numerous \nsea breeze showers/tstorms back in the forecast. After some\nwaffling the past few runs, guidance has come into far better\nagreement showing the front largely bisecting eastern NC by\nFriday, although a small minority still shows it stalling\nfarther north along the VA border. Wherever the boundary ends \nup setting up will be the focus for the highest risk of training\nshowers and thunderstorms contributing to a localized flash \nflooding threat. Forecast effective shear has trended down on \nFriday to generally less than 15 kt, so the risk of any stronger\nstorms is less than 5%.\n\nThe front lifts back north Saturday into Sunday as a shortwave\nmoves through the region. This shortwave will bring slightly\nbetter chances for showers and thunderstorms this weekend. Like\nFriday, flooding concerns remain the primary risk with PWATs\nstill around 2\" but effective shear slightly higher at 15-20 \nkt. Available instability remains a question mark for that day \ngiven potential convective evolution on Friday and the risk for \nlingering convective debris overnight and/or low stratus that \nmay develop on the cooler side of the frontal boundary. The NBM \nprobabilistic envelope has shifted slightly cooler for Saturday\nbut still remains high compared to both deterministic and MOS \nguidance, suggesting a cooler and therefore lower instability \nsolution will prevail. If more instability can develop, the \nmodest uptick in shear may provide a slightly more favorable \nenvironment for an isolated stronger storm or two.\n\nThere is higher confidence on a low- CAPE outcome Sunday. While\nheavy showers and some thunderstorms are possible with the \nshortwave, the severe threat appears to be more muted.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A more typical summertime pattern will settle in\nagain early next week as a large high sits over the southeast.\nBased on current model outputs, we have enough moisture and \ninstability present for some afternoon/evening sea breeze \nconvection early next week, but there are no larger scale\nsynoptic features of note that would promote any additional\nforcing.",
